  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nMostly clear skies were seen over SoCal with cumulus clouds across \nthe mountain areas. A trough is beginning to gain strength over the \nPacific NW as Tropical Storm Rachel churns 800 miles to the SW of \nSan Diego. The deepening troughing pattern over the West Coast will \nbring in more cooler air to the region by Saturday with a few \ndegrees cooler than today near the coast, closer to 10 degrees \ncooler for all other areas. Widespread 70s and 80s will be seen for \nareas below 6,000 feet with 90s in the lower deserts. The marine \nlayer will also build, bringing low clouds and less fog to coastal \nareas. \n\nTropical Storm Rachel is expected to make landfall somewhere near \nthe US/Mexico Border late Saturday night. While there remains some \nuncertainty in the track, tropical storm level impacts have the \npotential to impact San Diego County. A Tropical Storm Watch is in \neffect for all of San Diego County, including coastal flooding, \nwind, and periods of heavy rain Saturday night into Sunday. There is \nexpected to be a gradient in rainfall amounts with the higher \namounts on the southeastern side of the storm. There still remains \nsome uncertainty in the track, but a continued northern shift would \nincrease potential for heavy rain across the county with a southern \nshift making it more localized. Areas of flash and urban flooding \nare possible, especially where the maximum rainfall rates occur some \nmountain areas 0.50-1.00 inch. The San Diego River at Fashion Valley \nis currently forecast to peak at 7.4 feet (which is just below \nmonitor stage) Sunday afternoon. \n\nIncreased winds are expected with the onset and passage of Tropical \nStorm Rachel. Sustained winds of 39+ mph are expected on the \nsoutheast side of the storm. If the track continues to move further \nnorth, that would put more of San Diego county in a position to see \nwinds of that magnitude. Based on the current track, more southern \nparts of the county are most likely to see winds of this magnitude. \nRegardless, strong sustained winds and wind gusts can be expected on \nSunday. Winds may be enhanced by local terrain with gusts 40 to 55 \nmph on the desert slopes of the mountains and into the deserts. \nLocal gusts up to 60 mph possible on mountain ridges.\n\nTemperatures will continue to tumble on Sunday, dropping another 10 \ndegrees from those of Saturday. Models continue to differ on the \nexact path of where the trough to the north goes by early next week. \nAs of now, the coolest day of the week will be Monday with highs \nstruggling to reach 70 west of the mountains with 50s and 60s for \nplaces below 6,000 feet. Colder temperatures will also be expected \nfor the Big Bear region with highs in the 40s and lows in the 20s \nand 30s. Depending on the exact track of the low, chances for \nshowers continue into Monday, possibly Tuesday. Models are in fair \nagreement on trough beginning to lift out of the area by Wednesday \nas high pressure off the coast gains strength. This will lead to a \nwarmer and drier weather trend for the latter half of next week.",
